  "notes": "West leads the ♣5 against 3NT. South is fortunate to have escaped a diamond opening lead. West's choice of a club gives South a second club trick. That, along with 2 tricks in spades, 2 in hearts, and the ♦A, brings South's total up to 7.\n\nDummy's spades may provide an extra trick or two barring a bad break. The contract can be ensured, however, without depending on spades. South should turn his attention to clubs where the ♣K is the only high club out.\n\nOn most deals, declarer would be thrilled to win the first trick with a singleton queen in dummy. On this deal, if South lets dummy's ♣Q hold, the contract will be in danger.\n\nSouth requires 2 hand entries, 1 to knock out the ♣K and another to cash the long clubs. Unfortunately, there is only 1 outside entry, the ♦A.\n\nSouth must overtake dummy's ♣Q at trick 1 with the ♣A. After the ♣K is dislodged, the ♦A is an entry to the long clubs. South ends up taking 4 club tricks and making the contract.",
